.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1201 PM PDT Sat Sep 12 2026
(This evening through Sunday night)

As mostly sunny skies have returned to the region, we are
expected temperatures to warm into the 60s to lower 70s along the
northwest facing coastal locations and low to upper 70s around the
Santa Cruz region, bayshore, and coastal adjacent valleys.
Meanwhile, interior valleys are forecast to reach into the upper
70s to mid 80s with the warmest interior valleys of the Central
Coast reaching into the low 90s. Onshore wind gusts are forecast
to reach between 25 to 35 mph in the favored gaps, passes, and
coastal/higher elevation regions gusts up to 45 mph. This is ahead
of a dry frontal boundary which is expected to move through the
North Bay late this evening and then across the Central Coast
Sunday morning. Moderate to high confidence that low clouds will
not spread inland overnight into Sunday morning (patchy at best),
similar to this morning. However, the marine layer looks to hover
between 1,500 and 2,000 feet through the night.

By Sunday afternoon, the front is forecast to be south of the
region with cooler temperatures in the higher elevations while
slightly cooler for the lower elevations. Onshore winds will
increase once again tomorrow afternoon and evening with gusts
potentially exceeding those forecast for today, but only by 5 to
10 mph. Aloft, a light to gentle offshore breeze (with
occasionally moderate gusts) is forecast to develop in the higher
terrain as the mid/upper level trough axis shifts into the
northern Rockies. These winds are not forecast to be overly
strong, which will help limit fire weather concerns.

&&

.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1201 PM PDT Sat Sep 12 2026
(Monday through next Friday)

The trough axis is forecast to move into northern Plains early next
week, diminishing the offshore wind potential. Lingering troughiness
along the West Coast will maintain cooler than average temperatures
for much of the week ahead. As such, temperatures are forecast to
warm into the 60s to lower 70s along the northwest facing coastal
locations and low to upper 70s around the Santa Cruz region,
bayshore, and coastal adjacent valleys. Meanwhile, interior valleys
are forecast to reach into the upper 70s to mid 80s with the warmest
interior valleys of the Central Coast reaching into the low 90s.
Overnight and morning low clouds are expected to reach inland before
mixing out by the afternoon hours as well through the extended
forecast period.

