.FIRE WEATHER...
Issued at 1201 PM PDT Sat Sep 12 2026

The marine layer was around 1,500 feet in depth per 12Z OAK Upper
Air Sounding. However, stratus coverage did not match the typical
depth this morning and is not likely to again tonight despite the
marine layer forecast to be between 1,500-2,000 feet. A dry
frontal boundary is forecast to move across the Central Coast
tonight and into Sunday morning. Ahead of this boundary, we are
expecting an increase in 925 mb relative humidities which would
lead to higher humidity over the Timber and Plaskett fires
overnight and into Sunday morning. Sunday night into Monday night,
a light to gentle offshore breeze (with occasionally moderate
gusts) is forecast to develop in the higher terrain as the
mid/upper level trough axis shifts into the northern Rockies. As
such, areas above the marine layer often experience nocturnal
drying that will likely continue through early this upcoming week.


